Pages = {42-51},
Month = {MAY},
Abstract = {Unconventional oil and gas (UOG) extraction (fracking) has increased in
   the United States, as well as interest in the associated risks and
   benefits. This study's purpose was to qualitatively examine residents'
   perceptions about UOG development in their community. Fifteen
   interviewees involving residents of Garfield County, Colorado, a
   drilling-dense region, were transcribed and analyzed. The study found
   six themes: (1) health concerns, both human and animal, (2) power
   struggles between government and industry/between industry and
   residents, and (3) perception and some acceptance of increased risk.
   Less common themes were (4) reliance on science to accurately determine
   risk, (5) frustration with potential threat and loss of power, and (6)
   traffic and safety concerns. Community perceptions of UOG development
   are complex, and understanding the position of community members can
   support the need for additional public health research and impact
   assessments regarding community exposures from UOG drilling operation
   exposures.},
